  "output_text": "Introduction/Context\n- Experimental focus and dataset: The uploaded data come from IPMN-associated pancreatic neoplasms studied with global proteomics and glycoproteomics (IPMN vs ND). The global proteome differential data are in Wang_2025_Cancer_Cell_TableS2A.csv; the glycoproteome differential data are in Wang_2025_Cancer_Cell_TableS3I.csv. The IPMN cohort includes 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs, 5 IOPNs) and 76 macro-dissected normal ducts (NDs), with 32 cyst fluids analyzed as well.\n- Definitive signals in the provided data:\n  - Global proteome (TableS2A): A limited set of proteins show differential expression across IPMN vs ND in the previewed portion (example entries include NUDT4B and several immunoglobulin family genes). Several rows show log2 fold changes away from zero, but many entries have no Up/Down designation in the excerpt, indicating that not all proteins reach the significance threshold in the available subset.\n  - Glycoproteome (TableS3I): A robust set of glycopeptides are upregulated in IPMN vs ND, with numerous entries labeled Up.Sig (e.g., ADAM10, ENTPD5, CPD, MUC5AC glycopeptides, GALNT3, ITGA6, EPHA2, ERBB3, SLC12A2, TMEM63A, and many more). One clear downregulated glycopeptide is a TLR8 site (Down.Sig: log2FC negative, FDR ~0.0468). This pattern indicates pronounced glycosylation changes in IPMN, potentially independent of substantial global protein expression changes for some glycoproteins.\n- Not specified: exact IPMN subtype-specific signals beyond what is in TableS2A/TableS3I, complete mapping of glycopeptides to their corresponding global-protein fold changes beyond the provided preview, and full enrichment results (top terms) that would populate the complete pathway tables.\n\nKeywords\n- IPMN, ND, IPMN vs ND differential, global proteome, glycoproteome, TableS2A, TableS3I, Up.Sig, Down.Sig, mucins, MUC5AC, glycosylation, glycoproteins, adsorbable glycopeptides, immune/glycoprotein repertoire, epithelial adhesion molecules.\n\nCandidate Genes (mechanistically linked to extracted keywords)\n- MUC5AC (mucin, glycoprotein; major mucin in IPMN-associated surfaces)\n- ADAM10 (glycoprotein ectodomain sheddase; notable glycopeptide signal)\n- ENTPD5 (glycoprotein involved in nucleotide sugar metabolism and protein folding)\n- CPD (carboxypeptidase D; glycopeptide-bearing)\n- ITGA6 (integrin alpha-6; cell adhesion/glycoprotein context)\n- EPHA2 (Eph receptor; cell signaling/adhesion)\n- GALNT3 (glycosyltransferase for mucin-type O-glycosylation)\n- MUC5AC (already listed, but central in mucin biology)\n- ERBB3 (receptor tyrosine kinase; glycoprotein)\n- SLC12A2 (ion transporter; glycoprotein bearing)\n- TLR8 (Down.Sig glycopeptide; innate immune receptor; noted for Down signal)\n\nPathway Enrichments\n- Note: A formal, term-by-term enrichment table cannot be generated from the provided excerpt because explicit enrichment results (top KEGG/GO terms, leading-edge genes, and per-library counts) are not supplied in the data preview. The glycoproteome data show numerous Up.Sig terms dominated by mucin/glycosylation and cell-adhesion–related glycoproteins, with one Down.Sig term (TLR8). The global proteome preview shows fewer clearly labeled Up/Down entries and includes immunoglobulin genes, not enriched pathway terms.\n- Qualitative interpretation (based on available data):\n  - Glycoproteome (IPMN vs ND): Predominant upregulation of glycopeptides from mucins and glycosylation machinery (e.g., MUC5AC, GALNT3) and adhesion molecules (ITGA6, EPHA2, DSG2, ERBB3) suggests enhanced mucin-type glycosylation and alterations in cell–matrix/cell–cell interactions in IPMN tissue.\n  - Global proteome (IPMN vs ND): Fewer clearly significant changes in the subset shown; several immunoglobulin genes (IGLV/IGHV/IGKV) appear with variable log2FCs but only a few reach the Up status in the preview, implying more modest or selective changes at the protein-expression level in this sample slice.\n- In summary, glycosylation and mucin-associated glycopeptides show strong differential regulation in IPMN relative to ND, even in cases where global protein expression is not uniformly altered.\n\nTargeted (keyword-guided) subset\n- Filtered enrichment terms that would align with the extracted keywords (mucin-type glycosylation, glycoprotein processing, cell adhesion) could be highlighted if complete enrichment results were available. Based on the glycoproteome table, terms related to mucin biosynthesis/glycosylation and glycoprotein-mediated cell adhesion are the most plausible targeted themes, driven by MUC5AC, GALNT3, ITGA6, DSG2, EPHA2, EPHA receptors, and related glycopeptides.\n- If enrichment outputs were produced, the subset would emphasize:\n  - Mucin-type glycosylation pathways\n  - Glycoprotein processing and maturation\n  - Cell–cell adhesion and integrin signaling\n\nKey Genes/Proteins (leading edge)\nTwo compact tables summarize the leading-edge candidates implicated by the glycoproteome upregulation, with reference to the UpSigs (Up) and DownSigs (Down) observed in TableS3I. Where the global proteome data do not clearly show unchanged expression for a given glycoprotein in the provided subset, the rationale for “unchanged” status is not fully determinable from the 20-row preview; see notes below.\n\nUp (leading-edge glycopeptides; IPMN vs ND)\n| Gene | Direction | Role/Function | Pathway(s) |\n|------|-----------|---------------|------------|\n| MUC5AC | Up | Major mucin glycoprotein; mucosal barrier component | Mucin-type glycosylation; mucin biosynthesis; extracellular matrix interactions |\n| ADAM10 | Up | Metalloprotease; ectodomain shedding; proteolytic processing of receptors/glycoproteins | EGFR/Eph signaling cross-talk; cell adhesion; proteolysis of glycoproteins |\n| ENTPD5 | Up | Nucleotide sugar metabolism; chaperone-like role in protein folding | Glycosylation/ER protein folding; protein maturation |\n| CPD (carboxypeptidase D) | Up | Golgi/secretory pathway enzyme; glycopeptide-bearing | Glycoprotein processing; glycan maturation |\n| TMEM63A | Up | Mechanosensitive cation channel; membrane glycoprotein | Membrane transport; potential glycoprotein trafficking roles |\n| ITGA6 | Up | Integrin α6; cell–matrix adhesion | Cell adhesion; ECM receptor interaction |\n| EPHA2 | Up | Eph receptor tyrosine kinase | Ephrin signaling; cell positioning/adhesion |\n| GALNT3 | Up | Golgi GALNT enzyme; initiates mucin-type O-glycosylation | O-glycosylation; mucin-type glycosylation pathways |\n\nDown (leading-edge glycopeptides)\n| Gene | Direction | Role/Function | Pathway(s) |\n|------|-----------|---------------|------------|\n| TLR8 | Down | Toll-like receptor 8; innate immune sensing | Innate immune signaling; inflammatory responses |\n\nNotes on leading-edge tables:\n- The Up entries reflect glycopeptides with robust Up.Sig signals in TableS3I and include mucin-associated and adhesion/glycosylation-related proteins; the Down entry highlights the single Down.Sig glycopeptide (TLR8) observed in the excerpt.\n- Several other glycoproteins (e.g., EPHA2, ERBB3, EPHB3, SLC12A2, ERBB family members, DSC2, DSG2, MUC5AC, GALNT3) show strong glycopeptide-level upregulation in IPMN vs ND, suggesting coordinated changes in glycosylation and adhesion pathways, even when their global proteome status could not be confirmed as unchanged from the provided global proteome subset.\n\nComparisons (overlaps and discordances)\n- Global vs glycoproteome: The glycoproteome dataset reveals extensive upregulation of glycopeptides (Up.Sig) across multiple mucin, adhesion, and glycosylation-related proteins, whereas the global proteome subset shown includes fewer clearly significant changes and many immunoglobulin-related genes with variable fold changes. This pattern suggests glycosylation remodeling in IPMN tissues may occur without universal concomitant changes in protein abundance for all affected glycoproteins.\n- Tissue context and modality: The glycoproteome highlights post-translational remodeling (glycosylation) as a prominent feature of IPMN biology relative to ND, potentially reflecting altered secretory pathway activity and cell-surface glycoprotein presentation, even when total protein levels are unchanged or only modestly changed.\n\nImplications\n- Therapeutic/biomarker relevance: The prominent glycoproteome changes—especially mucin-related glycosylation (MUC5AC) and glycosylation machinery (GALNT3)—raise the possibility that glycosylation patterns, rather than total protein abundance, may better distinguish IPMN from ND and could serve as biomarkers or therapeutic targets focused on glycosylation processes or mucin-associated pathways.\n- Mechanistic interpretation: The upregulation of glycopeptides from ITGA6, EPHA2, ERBB3, and DSG2 suggests coordinated changes in cell adhesion and receptor signaling at the glycoprotein level, which may influence IPMN cell–cell interactions and microenvironment crosstalk.\n\nNext Steps & Clarification\n- Presence/absence check for glycoproteins with unchanged global expression: To firmly identify glycopeptides whose global protein expression is unchanged, map each glycopeptide’s parent protein to the full TableS2A differential results (log2FC, FDR). The current preview of TableS2A provides only a subset; a complete cross-tabulation would confirm which glycopeptides meet the “unchanged at global proteome” criterion.\n- Enrichment validation: Run a formal pathway enrichment analysis (KEGG, GO) on the subset of glycopeptides whose parent proteins are unchanged at the global level, to define the biological functions enriched specifically for glycosylation changes independent of protein abundance.\n- Experimental follow-up: Validate mucin-type glycosylation changes (e.g., MUC5AC glycoforms) in IPMN vs ND tissues, and assess whether these glycoforms correlate with IPMN subtype, stage, or progression risk.\n- Targeted follow-up questions:\n  - Do the mucin/glycosylation changes persist in matched IPMN samples from ND in other cohorts?\n  - Are the glycosyltransferases (e.g., GALNT family members) upregulated at the mRNA level or only in glycosylation signatures?\n  - Do the glycopeptide changes translate to distinct cyst fluid glycoforms that could serve as non-invasive biomarkers?\n\nReferences\n- No PMIDs are provided in the current prompt to validate through stepwise literature review. The conclusions here are strictly grounded in the provided TableS2A/TableS3I data (IPMN vs ND).\n\nIf you’d like, I can load the full TableS2A(TableS2A.csv) and TableS3I(TableS3I.csv) and perform a complete cross-matrix mapping (global vs glyco) to identify all glycopeptides with unchanged global expression, then deliver a fully annotated pathway table as soon as enrichment results are generated.",
